From 962ad07475d93ebad8d89fcd0c15d982135a7d41 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: lccc-perf <perf@localhost>
Date: Thu, 1 Oct 2026 09:34:37 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 4/8] x86: coalesce a loop induction variable that the
 allocator left in a slot

The register allocator is a windowed linear scan, so a value whose web spans
most of the function keeps its stack slot. In the LZ4 match-extension loop
that makes `ip` round-trip through memory once per iteration:

    .LBB14:
        movq 112(%rsp), %rcx     <- reload the IV
        movzbl (%rcx), %r8d
        cmpb (%rbx), %r8b
        jne .LBB16
    .LBB15:
        addq $1, %rcx
        movq  %rcx, 112(%rsp)    <- store it back
        addq $1, %rbx
        cmpq 56(%rsp), %rcx
        jb   .LBB14

Seven instructions, three touching memory. The store/reload pair is a
store-to-load forwarding round trip ON THE LOOP'S OWN RECURRENCE: ip cannot
advance faster than the ~5-cycle forward, so the IV chain and not the two
byte loads sets the throughput. Every oracle keeps both pointers in
registers.

Note what is NOT happening: %rcx is already live from the reload through the
increment to the store. The allocator never lost the register; it just
re-synchronised memory and register every iteration for nothing. So this
needs no register allocation at all - it hoists the reload onto the loop's
entry edge and sinks the store to the exits. Five instructions and one
memory op fewer per iteration.

The rewrite is TRANSPARENT TO THE LOOP BODY: the register holds the same
value at every program point inside the loop. Per iteration the original
establishes R = mem[slot], updates it and writes it back; the rewrite
establishes R once in the preheader and carries it. Nothing in the loop
writes the slot, so the two agree at loop entry and share every update in
between. The obligations are therefore about the slot, not the register:

  1. the slot's value at loop entry is its value in the preheader (nothing
     writes it in between, nothing in the loop writes it, its address is
     never taken so nothing can alias it);
  2. nothing else in the loop READS the slot - after the first iteration it
     holds the initial value, so a second reader would see a stale IV;
  3. every exit writes the slot with the final value;
  4. the reload is the header's first instruction and the header has a
     single fallthrough entry, which makes the hoist a move across a block
     boundary with nothing in between.

The one obligation the REGISTER side imposes is that nothing in the loop may
clobber it behind the pass's back: in the original the next iteration's
reload repaired any stray write, in the rewrite nothing does. That is what
`is_iv_chain_step` decides, and it is deliberately narrow.

Loop identification is dominator-based, not textual: back edges are edges
whose target dominates their source, and the natural loop is everything
reaching the latch without passing through the header. The conditional
branch and its fallthrough are tested as SEPARATE edges - reading "some
successor leaves the loop" classifies the latch's own back edge as an exit,
because the fallthrough beside it does leave.

Early exits route through a store block. When an exit already targets the
loop's fallthrough successor - the shape LZ4's `jne` has - the store block
is placed immediately before that block's label, so the branch is redirected
to the store's own label and falls straight through: one added mov, no extra
jump, on a path that leaves the loop once.

Generated exit labels are unique across the WHOLE unit, not the function:
lccc inlines, so one unit can hold several textual copies of the same callee
body and therefore of the same .LBB* labels, and two blocks sharing a label
are silently merged by the assembler.

Not gated on skip_phase4, which is unconditionally true outside MachInst
mode; that flag exists because the trampoline pass RENAMES registers, and
this pass renames nothing.

Corpus differential: 1 of 937 programs changes assembly, and it is the
target. 19 focused tests.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
