From 8ba6ad80f6fc0135b69557ae8112d0ae0caa6b65 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: lccc-perf <perf@localhost>
Date: Thu, 1 Oct 2026 10:42:53 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 5/8] x86: close two loop-shape gaps in the loop-IV spill
 coalescing pass

Red-team findings on the pass added in the previous commit. Both were found
by asking what the exit model does NOT see, not by a failing program.

1. A loop with two back edges was treated as two loops.

   Natural loops are per-latch, so with two latches the arm that reaches the
   OTHER latch is executed on every iteration but belongs to neither loop.
   Its register writes, and far worse its exits, were invisible. A loop is now
   the UNION of the natural loops of every back edge to its header.

2. A branch that is not a block's terminator but leaves the loop.

   The exit model is stated over terminators, so a conditional early exit
   emitted AHEAD of an unconditional jump was not an exit site at all. The pass
   would hoist the reload, delete the latch's store, and let that path leave
   carrying the PREVIOUS iteration's value. Now refused outright.

   This is the same shape as LZ4's `jne .LBB16`, which IS a terminator and is
   handled; the difference is structural, and the two tests
   `a_mid_block_branch_out_of_the_loop_is_refused` and
   `a_terminator_branch_to_a_loop_private_block_gets_a_store` pin both arms so
   the guard cannot be satisfied by refusing every header jump out of the loop,
   which would be 'correct' and would lose the whole optimization.

An earlier draft also added a reachability analysis to reject blocks the
header reaches that are private to the loop. It is removed: such a block is
either reached by a terminator, in which case the exit enumeration already
records it, or by a non-terminator, which finding 2 already refuses. The two
cases are exhaustive, the analysis could reject nothing the terminator guard
had not, and no test distinguished it from that guard. Redundant machinery with
no discriminating test is the same mistake as the liveness memo reverted in an
earlier round.

Both new refusal tests were verified to FAIL against the un-guarded pass and
pass against the guarded one, so they are regressions rather than assertions
that merely happen to hold.

Also corrects the label-uniqueness rationale. The original comment claimed
lccc's inliner duplicates callee-local labels in one unit, which is why exit
labels are searched across the whole unit. Measured: 0 of 935 corpus
assemblies contain a duplicated local label, and a forced always_inline
function called from two sites - fully inlined, no symbol, no remaining call -
emits 8 distinct labels and no duplicate. The emitter renumbers per unit. The
search stays (a collision is a silent miscompile and the cost is nil) but the
comment no longer cites a defect that does not exist.

Adds tests/bugs/loop_iv_spill_executes.sh: an end-to-end regression that
compiles the one corpus program where the pass fires with it on and off,
assembles each arm STANDALONE with as, links, runs both, and requires the
checksum 29f49acaab81f800 plus exactly one reload and one store. It fails if
the pass stops firing, so it cannot go vacuous.

Re-measured after these changes: lz4_match_extend -22.45%, ratio 0.7755,
11/11 paired wins, IQR [0.7647, 0.7955]. Corpus differential still 1 of 937.
3951 unit tests pass.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
