+# Loop-IV spill coalescing after the upstream byte-compare vectorizer
+
+## Rebase and scope
+
+Rebased the S14–S16 series onto `419307ca` (upstream `main`, including
+`d6e49e2b`, “Byte-compare window phase: vectorize two-stream lockstep byte
+scans”). The upstream change consumes LZ4's ordinary scalar match-extension
+loop earlier in the pipeline. The pre-rebase **−22.45%** LZ4 result is not a
+valid claim about the rebased default pipeline.
+
+This is exactly the kind of overlap a clean rebase should expose. I checked the
+pass instead of carrying forward the old headline.
+
+## Rebased measurements
+
+Compiler: `target/fastbuild/lccc`, fastbuild `-O1`; all A/B binaries from the
+same compiler, with only `CCC_PEEPHOLE_SKIP=loop_iv_spill,__none__` changing.
+Outputs are compared before any timing.
+
+| Pipeline / target | Assembly | Result |
+|---|---|---|
+| Default x86-64, byte-compare vectorizer enabled | pass-on and pass-off assemblies are byte-identical; no `.Lcciv_st` label | The loop-IV pass is not reached on this hot loop. An 11-pair timing run produced +4.23% / 3-of-11 wins, which is noise, not a regression or gain. |
+| Scalar fallback, `-mno-sse` | upstream vector arm is ineligible; loop-IV pass fires; standalone GNU-as/link/run regression passes | 11 alternating pairs: median **1,786.14 ms on / 2,179.21 ms off**, ratio **0.8196 (−18.04%)**, **11/11 wins**, paired-ratio IQR **[0.7798, 0.8623]**. Output is `29f49acaab81f800` in both arms. |
+
+Reproduce the measurement:
+
+```sh
+python3 scripts/benchmark_loop_iv_spill.py --reps 11
+```
+
+The runner first emits and checks both assembly arms (`.Lcciv_st` must occur
+only with the pass enabled), compiles both binaries, checks output identity,
+then alternates runtime samples. Its default `-mno-sse` is intentional: without
+that target constraint, the upstream vectorizer eliminates the scalar loop
+before this peephole can contribute. No hidden `LCCC_NO_BYTECMP_VEC` kill
+switch is used for the reported fallback result.
+
+The end-to-end assembler regression uses the same real scalar fallback:
+
+```sh
+LCCC=target/fastbuild/lccc \
+  SRC="$PWD/tests/benchmark/programs/lz4_compress.c" \
+  bash tests/bugs/loop_iv_spill_executes.sh
+```
+
+It independently assembles each arm with GNU `as`, links and runs both, checks
+the checksum, requires different assemblies, and checks exactly one reload and
+one store in the transformed arm.
+
+## Default-pipeline census
+
+The same-binary pass-on/off corpus differential on the rebased tree examined
+**940 programs**: 0 output assemblies changed; 1,876/1,880 arms built; the two
+failed source programs failed on both arms. Thus the pass is currently a
+specialized scalar-fallback improvement, not an optimization of the default
+vectorized LZ4 path. The earlier 1-of-937 result was superseded by this census.
+
+The upstream byte-compare arm's checked-in evidence is in
+`engineering/evidence/2026-10-01-bytecmp/`; it is independent of the scalar
+fallback result above. Keeping the loop-IV pass is justified by the measured
+−18.04% on the valid `-mno-sse` fallback and by its transparent scalar rewrite,
+not by attributing any benefit to the ordinary x86-64 vectorized path.
+
+## CI rework
+
+`ci_local.sh --fast` is now the single required fast gate set, and is also the
+default when no mode is specified. The GitHub workflow delegates to it once;
+its gate-parity check rejects a second YAML copy of Cargo build/test/clippy
+work. The old separate test, clippy, and oracle jobs rebuilt the compiler in
+three runners; the hosted Compiler Explorer gate now reuses the fast-suite
+compiler. The static codegen-quality gate was removed from `bench.yml` because
+it already runs once in the fast suite; the timed benchmark corpus remains its
+own distinct workload.
+
+The one Rust unit-test invocation now enables debug assertions, replacing the
+second all-targets test invocation with a separate profile. Host setup is one
+apt transaction and Cargo downloads use one cache action. Per-ref concurrency
+cancels validation of superseded commits. `lccc-snapshot.sh` accepts a matching
+`mode=fast` pass; there is no full/slow stamp requirement.
+
+A shared `ci_resources.sh` activates swap before selecting Cargo settings,
+caps parallelism at two, and tests its policy. A constrained host with under
+3 GiB RAM and under 2 GiB active swap uses one job, opt-level 0, no debuginfo,
+and no incremental state; the normal fastbuild profile is retained once the
+machine has sufficient RAM or swap. The fallback affects the Rust compiler
+executable's build profile only, not the generated C code.
